# LICENSED DATA — present locally, NEVER committed. This repository is PUBLIC.
# `lexicons/external/` holds sources we have access to and may not redistribute:
# worddb.byu.txt is BNC/COCA/COHA/SOAP frequencies and BYU corpora are purchased
# access with redistribution prohibited. Copied here rather than read from
# ~/Dropbox so the CODE PATH is repo-relative instead of machine-specific — the
# file's location stops being one person's filesystem — while git never sees it.
#
# A clone therefore has the code and not the data, which is correct and is
# exactly why `lexicons/EXTERNAL.md` requires that a missing source REFUSE rather
# than degrade. An ignored file is invisible; a silently smaller answer is worse.
lexicons/external/

# `data/` IS IGNORED AGAIN, WITH NAMED EXCEPTIONS (RH, 2026-08-20, second ruling).
#
# The first ruling today removed `data/` wholesale -- "we dont care if prompts are
# committed" -- and that was about the transgressive battery. But `data/` mostly
# does not hold prompts; it holds FLEET STATE, and un-ignoring it exposed run
# plans and live cloud bookkeeping that nobody meant to track. `cloud_state.json`
# in particular mutates on every fleet run, so committing it snapshots a moving
# object and churns the history.
#
# So: ignore the directory's CONTENTS and name what is wanted.
#
# **THE TRAILING SLASH MATTERS AND IS WHY THIS IS `data/*`.** `data/` excludes the
# DIRECTORY, git never descends into it, and a `!data/thing.json` below it is
# silently inert -- it does not error, it just never matches. `data/*` excludes
# the contents instead, which is the only form under which the negations work.
# Verified with `git check-ignore -v` on every path below, not assumed.
data/*
# the CJK dictionary IS the instrument: dict_sha b16011275c42955c is computed from
# it, and a checkpoint measured against a different dict is a different measurement
!data/dict/
!data/dict/**
# fleet artifacts another seat committed at 41075e8 under the first ruling, kept
# tracked deliberately: rates are a measurement, the blocklist and the shard plan
# are the record of what a run was pointed at
!data/fleet_shards.json
!data/cloud_bad_machines.json
!data/model_twp_rates.jsonl
#
# NOT un-ignored, and each for its own reason rather than by omission:
#   data/cloud_state.json   live, rewritten every fleet run
#   data/pilot_shard.json   superseded by fleet_shards.json
#   data/pilot2_plan.json   a plan for a run already made
#   data/pos_context/       a hashstash CACHE of spaCy POS tags keyed on
#                           (tagger, prompt, word), 22.8 MB here and 190 MB in the
#                           archive per malignment/pos.py, with a `__written_at__`
#                           on every row. Regenerable by re-tagging, which is the
#                           test that makes it a cache and not data.
*.lock


# Scratch work does not live in the repo — see experiments/README.md
sandbox/

# regenerable from rating_items.json + seed (run.py --stage assemble)
experiments/sex_violence_lexicon/results/blocks/

# ── the app ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ui/node_modules/
ui/.svelte-kit/
# **UN-IGNORE `ui/src/lib/`.** Line 17 of this file is a bare `lib/` from the
# Python template, meaning a virtualenv's lib directory -- and it matches
# `ui/src/lib/` just as well, which is SvelteKit's `$lib` alias and holds every
# component plus the whole client API surface.
#
# Caught 2026-08-16 by running `git status --untracked-files=all ui/` before
# staging and noticing six files that should have been listed were not. **Nothing
# would have failed**: `git add ui/` succeeds, the commit reads as complete, and
# the repo ends up holding an app whose every `import ... from '$lib/...'`
# resolves to nothing. Absence of data must read as absence, never as completion
# -- the same rule the runner applies to skipped prompts, one layer out.
#
# A rule written for one language's conventions silently governing another's is
# not something any check in this repo would have raised, and the same trap is
# waiting for `bin/`, `include/` and `share/` if a future directory uses them.
!ui/src/lib/
!ui/src/lib/**
# BUILD OUTPUT, DELIBERATELY NOT TRACKED. The archive tracked its `ui_dist` so a
# clone could serve the app without npm, and paid for it in diff noise: every
# rebuild rewrites hashed asset filenames, so the history records rebuilds and
# not changes. This repo already separates authored from derived -- ClickHouse is
# "derived, rebuildable" and is not in git either. `ui/README.md` says how to
# build it, which is one command.
malignment/ui_dist/

# Playwright's per-run output: traces, actual/diff images, the HTML report. The
# SNAPSHOTS under `ui/e2e/**-snapshots/` are tracked on purpose -- they are the
# baseline the suite compares against, so an untracked one makes the check
# vacuous on a fresh clone.
# Playwright output. PATH-ANCHORED AND THEREFORE WRONG: the rule was
# `ui/test-results/`, so a run started from the repo root wrote an unignored
# test-results/ beside it and git offered it for commit. Same failure as the
# national_story rules on 2026-09-02 -- an anchored ignore does not fail by
# hiding something, it fails by exposing it.
**/test-results/
ui/playwright-report/
ui/blob-report/

# Pre-digested position cells: `f21.json` + `m03.json` (60 MB of JSON) as one
# 11.5 MB parquet, rebuilt in about a second by `institutional/digest.py`.
# Derived and rebuildable, so it follows the rule above -- and deliberately NOT a
# witness: every producer keeps reading the JSON, which is what lets `verify()`
# check the digest against a booked artifact rather than against itself.
experiments/slot_ratings/institutional/results/base_side/position_cells.parquet

# verse_capacity figures are regenerated by producers/m05_capacities_overview.py
experiments/emergence/capacities/figures/

# norm_change long tables live in ~/malignment-data/norm_change (3.0 GB)
experiments/displacement/norm_change/results/

# national_story generated data lives in $MALIGNMENT_DATA/national_story
# PATH-ANCHORED RULES BREAK WHEN THE EXPERIMENT MOVES, AND THEY BREAK TOWARD
# COMMITTING. national_story moved to passage_analysis/ on 2026-09-02 and every
# rule below stopped matching at once -- git then offered the 183k-span sqlite
# SYMLINK as a new file to track, which is the direction a stale ignore always
# fails in. Written unanchored so the next move is free.
**/national_story/*.jsonl
!**/national_story/prompts_*.jsonl
**/national_story/results/story_*.jsonl
# conflict.sqlite is a SYMLINK to the data dir, with a machine-specific absolute
# target -- committing it would hand everyone else a broken path, and the other
# machine's user is `ryan`, not `rj416`. Recreate with
#   ln -sfn ~/malignment-data/national_story/conflict.sqlite \
#           experiments/passage_analysis/national_story/conflict.sqlite
# or just rebuild: python experiments/passage_analysis/national_story/export_db.py
**/national_story/conflict.sqlite
